  "explanation": {
    "short": "Two small marks sit above a consonant and bend the tone: ่ (mai ek) and ้ (mai tho). On a mid-class letter, ่ makes the tone low and ้ makes it fall.",
    "more": "Low is a low, flat voice. Falling starts high and drops, like an emphatic \"No!\". Look at ป่า (forest, low) and ป้า (aunt, falling): the letters are the same and only the mark differs, so the mark is the whole difference between two words. The tone mark is written over the first consonant or over the vowel that follows it (ตู้, บ้าน). The rule you have just learned is only for mid-class letters; low-class and high-class letters will treat the marks differently."
  },
